@node Gowl Bar Slots
@chapter Gowl Bar Slots: Bar Items Elisp Creates and Fills

@cindex bar slots
@cindex gowl bar, slots
@cindex gowl bar, items from Elisp
@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set
@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-remove

Every widget on the gowl bar finds out what to show by itself: the
clock reads the time, the cpu widget reads @file{/proc}.  A @dfn{slot}
is told.  It is a bar item with a name, created at run time by
something outside the bar, whose text, glyph, colour, hover text and
urgency are pushed whenever they change, and whose clicks are reported
back to whoever made it.

@file{cmacs-gowl-bar-slots.el} is how Elisp makes one:

@lisp
(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set "mail" :text "3 unread" :role 'peach
                         :on-click (lambda (_click) (mu4e)))
(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set "mail" :text "4 unread")   ; only the text changes
(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-remove "mail")
@end lisp

No restart, no configuration reload and no entry in a widget list: the
slot appears when it is set and is gone when it is removed.  It needs
@command{cmacs --gowl} with the bar loaded; anywhere else every
function here does nothing and returns @code{nil}.

Three things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{Nothing a slot carries is run by the compositor.}  A slot has no
command.  An @code{:on-click} function stays in Emacs; the bar only
reports that a click happened, and Emacs calls the function, from a
timer, on its own thread.
@item
@emph{A colour is a role, never a hex value.}  @code{:role 'peach}
follows the session palette like the rest of the bar.  @code{:role
"#fab387"} is an error in Elisp and refused by the bar, so a slot
cannot be left the wrong colour by a theme change.
@item
@emph{A slot that outlives its owner is a bar that lies}, so slots have
owners, and a way of going away on their own.  @xref{Gowl Bar Slot
Lifetime}.
@end itemize

The compositor's half---the slot table, the @code{slot} plugin that
draws one, the IPC commands and JSON-RPC methods that standalone gowl
users get---is documented in gowl's @file{docs/bar.org}, @cite{Slots}.
This chapter is the Elisp.

