@node Gowl Bar Slot Setting
@section Setting a Slot

@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-id
@vindex cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-roles
@vindex cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-max-text

@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set @var{id} &rest @var{plist}} creates the
slot @var{id} or changes it.  @var{id} is a string or a symbol of
letters, digits, @samp{_}, @samp{.} and @samp{-}, starting with a
letter, a digit or @samp{_} (@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-id} makes one
out of any name).  Only the keys @var{plist} names change; the rest of
the slot stays as it was.

@table @code
@item :text
What it says.  @code{nil} or @code{""} shows nothing---a slot with
neither text nor icon takes no room.  Default: empty.
@item :icon
A glyph drawn before the text, in the bar's icon font (what
@code{button.icon} takes).
@item :role
A bar theme role, symbol or string: @code{text}, @code{accent},
@code{red}, @code{green}, @code{yellow}, @code{peach} @dots{}
(@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-roles}).  Default: the urgency's own.
@item :tooltip
Hover text.  Default: the id.
@item :urgency
@code{low} (drawn muted), @code{normal}, or @code{critical} (the red
role, over a wash of it).  Default @code{normal}.
@item :region
@code{left}, @code{center} or @code{right}.  Default @code{right}.
@item :order
An integer: where among the region's slots.  Negative sorts before the
region's own widgets, zero and up after them.  Default 0.
@item :bar
@code{top} or @code{bottom}.  Default @code{top}.
@item :output
The one output to show on (@code{"eDP-1"}); @code{nil} for all of them.
@item :on-click
A function of one argument.  @xref{Gowl Bar Slot Clicks}.
@item :owner
A symbol naming the package the slot belongs to, for
@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-clear}.
@item :ttl
Seconds after the last push at which the bar removes the slot by
itself.  Default: never.
@end table

It returns non-@code{nil} when the bar took the slot now, and
@code{nil} when no bar is running or the bar refused---which is
reported in the echo area, once per slot and reason, not on every push.
It signals an error only for a @var{plist} that could never be right:
an unknown key, a role that is not a role, an order that is not an
integer.  That kind of mistake is better found where it was written
than reported from a timer.

The text is made one line and cut to
@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-max-text} columns (120) with an ellipsis.
The bar has its own, larger limit of 256 bytes.

@subheading Where a slot goes

A slot is not in any widget list.  That is what lets one be added to a
bar somebody else configured---and what keeps it there, because a
configuration replaces a region's @emph{widgets} and leaves its slots
alone.  @code{:region} and @code{:order} say ``at the start'' or ``at
the end'' of a region:

@lisp
(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set "task" :region 'left :order 10
                         :text "write the report")
(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set "build" :region 'right :order -1
                         :text "building")
@end lisp

In the right region ``before'' means nearer the screen edge, as it does
in @code{widgets-right}, which reads outward from the edge.

To put a slot @emph{between} two particular widgets, give it a place in
the bar's own list and the slot of that id fills it:

@lisp
(cmacs-gowl-bar-configure-top
 '(("widgets-left" . "tags slot:task title")))
(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set "task" :text "write the report")
@end lisp

A listed @code{slot:@var{id}} sits where the list says; the slot's own
@code{:bar}, @code{:region} and @code{:order} then no longer apply, and
removing the slot empties the place without taking it out of the list.

@node Gowl Bar Slot Clicks
@section Clicks

@cindex bar slots, clicks
@vindex cmacs-gowl-module-event-functions

A slot with @code{:on-click} is clickable.  The function is called with
one argument, a plist:

@table @code
@item :id
The slot's id, a string.
@item :button
@code{left}, @code{right}, @code{middle}, or @code{button-@var{n}} for
anything else.
@item :modifiers
A list of @code{shift}, @code{ctrl}, @code{alt} and @code{super};
@code{nil} for none.
@item :output
The name of the output it was clicked on, or @code{nil}.
@end table

@lisp
(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set
 "mail" :text "3 unread"
 :on-click (lambda (click)
             (pcase (plist-get click :button)
               ('left  (mu4e))
               ('right (cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set "mail" :text "")))))
@end lisp

How it gets there: the bar announces @samp{slot-click @var{id}
@var{button} @var{modifiers} @var{output} @var{owner}} as a module
event, cmacs carries the compositor's @code{module-event} signal to
Emacs's thread and runs @code{cmacs-gowl-module-event-functions}, and
this file's listener looks the slot up and schedules the function with
a zero-second timer.  So the function does @emph{not} run inside the
compositor's signal dispatch: it may prompt, pop a buffer or signal an
error like any command, and an error is reported in the echo area
rather than taking the hook down.  A click on a slot that is not
Elisp's---another owner's, or one made from the socket---is ignored
here.

A slot without @code{:on-click} is not a button; a press on it falls
through as it does on a label.

@node Gowl Bar Slot Definitions
@section Slots That Keep Themselves Up to Date

@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-define-slot
@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-refresh

@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-define-slot @var{id} &rest @var{plist}} is for an
init file.  @var{id} is an unquoted symbol (or a string).  It takes
every key above and two more:

@table @code
@item :update
A function of no arguments giving what the slot shows: a string (its
text), @code{nil} (nothing), or a plist of slot keys to change.
@item :every
Call it again this many seconds apart.  Without it the function runs
once, and again on @code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-refresh}.
@end table

@lisp
(cmacs-gowl-bar-define-slot clock-in
  :region 'left :role 'accent
  :update (lambda ()
            (and (bound-and-true-p org-clock-current-task)
                 (substring-no-properties org-clock-current-task)))
  :every 30
  :on-click (lambda (_click) (org-clock-goto)))

(cmacs-gowl-bar-define-slot unsaved
  :update (lambda ()
            (let ((n (cl-count-if (lambda (b)
                                    (and (buffer-file-name b)
                                         (buffer-modified-p b)))
                                  (buffer-list))))
              (when (> n 0)
                (list :text (format "%d unsaved" n)
                      :urgency (if (> n 5) 'critical 'normal)))))
  :every 20)
@end lisp

Evaluating the form again @emph{replaces} the slot: a key the new form
no longer has is gone, and the old timer is cancelled.  It works before
the compositor has started; the slot is remembered and shown when the
bar is.

@strong{The update function runs from a timer in Emacs---under
@command{cmacs --gowl} on the thread the whole desktop waits on.  It
must return at once.}  Read state Emacs already has.  Never run a
process, read the network or prompt in it.  For anything that has to be
computed elsewhere, set the slot from that computation's callback with
@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set} instead; an LQL watch's @code{:bar}
option does exactly that, and is the easy way to put a @emph{query} on
the bar (@pxref{LQL Watch Bar}).

An update function that signals is reported once and leaves the slot as
it was.

@node Gowl Bar Slot Lifetime
@section Lifetime

@cindex bar slots, owners
@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-restore
@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-clear
@vindex cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-owner

Every slot Elisp makes is remembered in a table, and carries the owner
tag @code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-owner} (@code{"cmacs"}) on the bar.
Between them those two do the following.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{Before the bar exists.}  A slot set before
@code{cmacs-gowl-mode} has started is remembered and pushed by
@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-restore}, which the mode calls once its bar
is up.
@item
@emph{When the compositor restarts.}  The same call puts every slot
back after a @code{gowl-stop} and @code{gowl-start}.  It begins by
asking the bar to drop everything carrying the tag, so what the bar
shows afterwards is exactly what the table holds---nothing an earlier
Emacs state left behind.
@item
@emph{When the configuration reloads.}  Nothing to do: slots are not
part of a widget list, and a reload does not touch them.
@item
@emph{When a package goes away.}  @code{(cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-clear
'my-package)} removes every slot made with @code{:owner 'my-package}.
With no argument it removes every slot Elisp has made.
@item
@emph{When Emacs stops pushing.}  @code{:ttl 180} has the @emph{bar}
remove the slot three minutes after the last push to it.  Every push is
a heartbeat, so a slot refreshed once a minute stays as long as its
timer runs and no longer.
@item
@emph{Somebody else's slot of the same id.}  The bar lets only a slot's
owner change it.  A push to an id that a script on the IPC socket made
is refused and reported, not silently fought over, and
@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-remove} names the tag so it never removes one
that is not cmacs's.
@end itemize

The bar holds at most 64 slots.

@node Gowl Bar Slot Functions
@section Functions and Options

@findex cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-list

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set @var{id} &rest @var{plist}
Create or change a slot; non-@code{nil} when the bar took it.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-remove @var{id}
Forget the slot and take it off the bar; non-@code{nil} if there was
one.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-list &optional @var{live}
The slots Elisp has made, as @code{(@var{id} . @var{plist})} by id.
With @var{live}, what the bar is showing for cmacs, parsed from its
JSON.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-clear &optional @var{owner}
Remove one package's slots, or all of them; returns how many.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-define-slot @var{id} &rest @var{plist}
Macro: define a slot in an init file, optionally with @code{:update}
and @code{:every}.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-refresh &optional @var{id}
Command: run the @code{:update} function of one slot, or of every slot
that has one.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-id @var{name}
@var{name} as a valid slot id; the same name always gives the same id.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-restore
Show every remembered slot on the bar that is running now.  Called by
@code{cmacs-gowl-mode}.
@end table

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-owner
The owner tag every slot made from Elisp carries on the bar.  Default
@code{"cmacs"}.
@item cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-max-text
Longest text, in columns, a slot is given.  Default 120.
@end table

@node Gowl Bar Slot Primitives
@section The Primitives

@findex gowl-bar-slot-set
@findex gowl-bar-slot-remove
@findex gowl-bar-slot-clear
@findex gowl-bar-slot-list

Four DEFUNs in @file{cmacs/gowl/cmacs-gowl-bar-slots.c} sit under the
functions above.  Each sends one command to the bar module---the path
every other bar DEFUN takes---with the gowl lock held for the call, and
returns the bar's reply as a string, or @code{nil} when no compositor
is running.

@table @code
@item gowl-bar-slot-set @var{id} @var{fields}
@code{"created @var{id}"}, @code{"updated @var{id}"} or @code{"error:
@var{reason}"}.
@item gowl-bar-slot-remove @var{id} &optional @var{owner}
@code{"removed @var{id}"} or @code{"error: @var{reason}"}.
@item gowl-bar-slot-clear @var{owner}
@code{"removed @var{n}"}; @var{owner} @code{t} removes every slot.
@item gowl-bar-slot-list &optional @var{owner}
A JSON array.
@end table

@var{fields} is an alist of @code{(@var{key} . @var{value})} with
@var{key} a string or symbol and @var{value} a string, an integer, or
@code{t} / @code{nil} for true and false.  The request is built as a
JSON object in C, so no text---a mail subject, a task heading, whatever
a query returned---can break out of its field.  They exist rather than
going through @code{gowl-run-command} for that reason, and because a
command run that way is announced to every IPC subscriber, which for a
status that changes every few seconds would be most of what a
subscriber hears.

Use the Elisp functions rather than these: a slot set with the
primitive is not remembered, its clicks are not dispatched, and it is
not put back when the compositor restarts.

In an Emacs built before the primitives,
@file{cmacs-gowl-bar-slots.el} falls back to @code{gowl-run-command}
with the same bar commands.

@node Gowl Bar Slot Tests
@section Tests

@vindex cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-command-function

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-tests.el} needs no bar: the one
function that would reach one,
@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slots-command-function}, is rebound to record what
it was given.  It covers a push carrying the whole slot, bounded
one-line text, every bad argument signalling and sending nothing, ids
from arbitrary names, no bar and a refusing bar (said once), removal by
owner, the click line's parsing and its deferred dispatch (never inside
the event; never for somebody else's slot), a failing handler
contained, @code{cmacs-gowl-bar-define-slot} replacing and timing
itself, and a restore clearing before it pushes.  The C half is held by
source to the gowl lock rules---one function calls into gowl, takes the
scoped lock first and leaves only through @code{unbind_to}.  The
primitives themselves are exercised only for their argument checks,
with no compositor running.

The slot table, the limits and placement are tested in gowl, against
the real bar module: @file{deps/gowl/tests/test-bar-slots.c}.
